FWIW here&apos;s a curl example that demonstrates the resulting encoding:

{code}
curl -s -e &quot;&lt;script&gt;alert(&apos;argh&apos;)&lt;/script&gt;&quot; -X POST http://localhost:8080/DOESNT_EXIST
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Error while processing /DOESNT_EXIST&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Error while processing /DOESNT_EXIST&lt;/h1&gt;
...
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Referer&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;alert(&amp;apos;argh&amp;apos;)&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&quot;id=&quot;Referer&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;alert(&amp;apos;argh&amp;apos;)&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
...
    &lt;/body&gt;
{code}

the escaping changed the readability of the response, because it now escapes the &lt;pre&gt; and &lt;br&gt; tags. which now don&apos;t look nice in the browser anymore.
the intention of the xhtml response was that it is human and machine readable. further, it alters the DOM of the chagelog entry which might cause backward compatibility. 

IMO it would be better to only escape the actual changelog entries instead of the entire changelog.

eg:
&lt;td&gt;ChangeLog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;div id=&quot;ChangeLog&quot;&gt;
&amp;lt;pre&amp;gt;created(&quot;/foo.txt&quot;);&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;created(&quot;/bar.txt&quot;);&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/pre&amp;gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;

